The honest limitation of raw frequency, even after stopword filtering:

"Claude" appearing 102 times doesn't mean 102 decisions about Claude — it means Claude is the subject of the whole conversation, which you already knew. Raw frequency tells you the topics of the conversation, which is useful context, but it doesn't by itself tell you where a decision happened. It's a coarse signal, not a precise one.

That's exactly why this is most useful combined with the other patterns we just listed, not standing alone:

A term spiking suddenly in a short window (frequency localized to messages 40-55, not spread evenly) is a much stronger signal than overall frequency — it suggests a concentrated discussion, not just a recurring topic.

A term appearing alongside decision-signal words ("auth" + "decided" in the same message) is stronger than either alone.

